-**Formation, persistence, transformation, degeneration and dissolution are relational changes along runs, not five stages.** One activity can make a continuation actually possible while destroying another condition. A form that persists need not keep its former capacity to act. The following results establish these distinctions at their scopes.
-
-- **Endpoint possibilities.** Along an actual trajectory, every endpoint possibility is kept exactly where a return exists; otherwise there is a constructive witness of loss ([endpoint-loss-and-return](·); an earlier module outside the spine, with no axioms).
-- **Current capacity and eventual reachability.** The two differ: preparation changes what can be jointly realized within one step ([effective-capacity-and-eventual-reachability](·); periphery; standard Lean axioms).
-- **The whole space of possibilities.** Pooling the steps of a family of processes keeps exactly the union of their endpoint histories exactly where that union is closed under joining at a shared boundary. Containing every outcome is not the condition under which outcomes can be spliced at will ([becoming-context-coherence](·); periphery; standard Lean axioms).
-- **Joint activity.** The joint activity can change while every part's local state stays the same ([joint-correlation-reduced-dynamics](·); periphery; standard Lean axioms). A local distinction can be lost and still be recovered within a retained joint relation ([control-coherence-loss](·); periphery; standard Lean axioms).
-- **Law and trajectory.** The law of steps and the endpoint trajectories determine each other ([trace-law-equivalence](·)); maps preserving steps transport trajectories ([finite-trace-transport](·)). Both are earlier modules outside the spine, with no axioms.
-- **Laws with no premise.** The following hold of every unfolding, with no premise about it ([Becoming.Change](·), [coming-and-ceasing-keep-pace](·)). A determination comes to hold or ceases to hold across a step. The arising and ceasing of a formation, the forming and dissolving of a dependence, and a condition coming to stand and ceasing to stand are this one change, read of activity, dependence and standing. Over any stretch, an amount at the end with the falls between equals the amount at the start with the rises between. Amounts do not go below zero, so in a finite family recurring falls need recurring rises: in every unfolding, finitely many conditions that keep falling keep rising; with amounts bounded, recurring rises also need recurring falls. So in a finite family coming to be and ceasing to be keep pace. Over any stretch, the members holding at the end with those that ceased between are exactly the members holding at the start with those that came between; the difference is at most the number of members, and each recurs exactly when the other does. With finitely many formations and activity decided, arising recurs exactly when ceasing does: some formation arises at arbitrarily late steps exactly where some formation ceases at arbitrarily late steps. A condition coming to stand recurs exactly where its ceasing to stand recurs, with no premise at all. Finiteness does the work: endless novelty can arise without ceasing (`Accretion`), and endless initial members can cease without arising (`Attrition`). Every law below states the premise it adds, and the premises carve parts of the whole space. The method commitments of the representation are disclosed separately: discrete steps and natural-number amounts (section 0). Decidedness when counting is not among them: it is a condition the counting laws carry (section 0, block B).
-- **Change of dependence.** Dependence itself belongs to the unfolding. Which conditions a formation depends on, and how much it needs, can form, hold, break and dissolve across steps, and each change happens at some step ([Becoming.Dependence](·)). Under the premise that a formation is active only where supported (`Dependent`, which excludes buffering and lag), theorems connect three outcomes of a loss. They neither exhaust nor exclude one another. Continuing with a dependence no weaker than before means the condition was restored. Staying active after a loss with no return means the dependence was transformed. With the dependence unchanged, the formation ends. In one process, from one configuration, after the same dependence breaks at the same step (read by the convention of the step's start), all three outcomes can occur. The reading of the break does not fix the successor configuration; the step that constitutes the break selects the outcome ([becoming-dependence-dynamics](·)). `Dependent` is exactly the zero reserve. A reserve does not rise while the formation is unsupported, and falls while it is active then. With a reserve, a formation can be active beyond its support at most as many times as its reserve allows. Endings after a loss, and coextinction, are deferred, not escaped. Under a decaying reserve, coextinction runs down a chain of dependence of any length, a finite chain such as a food chain included, within a bound at each link. Without decay, with excluded middle as a hypothesis, every relatum on the chain still ends, with no bound. The reserve can be the level of a held condition, and the same unfolding can also be read as dependence transformed onto the held condition ([Becoming.Reserve](·), [reserve-defers-ending](·)). These are definitions and constraints within the representation, not a proof of the one-sentence statement.
-- **Identity across steps.** Which formation at one step continues which formation at the step before is fixed by the representation's naming; the relations at each step do not fix it ([Becoming.Identity](·), [identity-across-steps](·)).
-  - *What naming carries.* Renaming preserves the readings defined by relations; five were checked. Labels carry nothing; the pattern of names across steps carries identity. The relations at each step, each condition's class, the number of takers and “more” do not depend on identity across steps. A formation's class over a run, the tenure and undermining of a dependence, the difference between transformation and “one ends and another forms”, arising, and unceasing arising do. Changing only who continues whom changes all of these (`Turnover`). Arising produced by naming alone can meet every premise of continued generation below (`TideFlip`).
-  - *Re-identification against an old naming.* A new naming that keeps the names of continued activity and continued dependence across steps, relative to the old naming, creates no arising and removes no undermining. It can still join a formation that ends to one that begins at or after that step: succession. A lineage arises unceasingly when its members are named apart, and has no arising when they are named as one (`Lineage`). Where a successor continues a predecessor, drawing after the predecessor's loss undermines at every step (`Succession`). Keeping continuity both ways keeps arising and undermining exactly and excludes succession within one step (`no_join`), but succession across dormancy still changes a formation's class (`Dormancy`).
-  - *Wholes.* A lineage and its members are two levels of one unfolding. One relation of parts and whole is stated by the whole: a whole is active exactly while some part is. This is a coincidence of activity, not constitution; a lineage is the whole of its members ([Becoming.Wholes](·), [whole-persists-through-generation](·)). Take a whole active at every step from some step on. Suppose its parts are brief (never active at two steps d apart), or its parts end with finitely many active at once (all activity ends and the active parts can be listed). Then its parts cannot stop arising: from no step on is it true that none arises. The whole persists through the generation of its parts. With brief parts this can be the same parts arising again (`Layered`); with ending parts it needs ever new parts (`needs_new`): from no step on can parts outside any given list stop arising. Neither is growth in number. With a part that never ends, or with infinitely many held in reserve at once, this fails (`Immortal`, `Countless`); and parts arising unceasingly do not by themselves keep the whole going (`Gaps`). The Lean conclusions here are negations of universal statements, not named steps of arising.
-  - *Succession and 生生不息.* Succession is read through the formed relations of continuation, and 生生不息 (shēngshēng bùxī, “life giving rise to life without ceasing”) is read at the level of the whole: 不息 as arising that recurs through renewal, and 生生不息 as 生生 going on with more still able to form, so that a whole which only returns, copying forms it has had, has 不息 but not 生生不息 [AI reading, D5].
-  - *More forcing arising.* Under a census, with `present` listing each formation once and only active formations taking (`Taking`), the unceasing arising forced by “more” holds under every one-to-one naming (`More.unceasing_reidentified`). Under every continuation in which no formation continues as two, more also forces arising (`More.unceasingFrom`; merges allowed). No split is sufficient, not necessary: one split need not defeat arising (`Fork`), and reading new formations as budding defeats the conclusion (`Budding`). Without a census, no split is not enough (`Dormant`). Across one step the condition is exact: whatever the formations before and after, more forces arising exactly when no formation continues as two. Under every continuation with no split, arising is forced exactly when there are more after the step than before (`forces_iff_noSplit`, `forced_iff_more`).
-  - *The census under continuation.* The census laws do not depend on the identity names fix. Under a continuation, a formation ceases where no active formation after the step continues it. Under a census, without splits, the formations active after the step with those that ceased are at most those active before with those that arose; without merges, at least; with neither, exactly. So without splits recurring ceasing needs recurring arising, and without merges under a bounded count the converse. Take one unfolding with two formations at each step. Read as each formation continuing only itself, it has one arising and one ceasing at each step. Read as budding, ceasing recurs and nothing ever arises; read as joining, arising recurs and nothing ever ceases (`Census.balanceFrom`, `Tandem`, [census-balance-under-continuation](·)).
-- **Two roles of one relatum.** Formations and conditions are two roles of one relatum, not two kinds of being ([Becoming.Roles](·), [life-as-condition-of-life](·)). The two-kind representation embeds in the one-kind reading in which no formation is a condition. Read so, every listed reading and the connection of valuations stay the same; a formation that was ever active there is neither embodied (standing as a condition exactly while it is active) nor bears on itself. The one-kind reading needs one more correspondence: which condition is which formation. It is supplied by the shared index, a premise of the representation, and the two-kind reading cannot check it (`relabel`). Embodiment and bearing on oneself are readings under this correspondence. Where activity needs support (`Dependent`), a formation depending on an embodied relatum is active only while that relatum is. Activity passes along chains of dependence, and a relatum's ending ends every formation whose chain of dependence reaches it (`coextinct`). The arising of an embodied relatum is the rise of its level as a condition, so generation is itself replenishment ([generation-is-replenishment](·)).
