-**What listing once commits to.** A list can be listed once exactly where identity among its members is decided (`listsOnce_iff_decided`); its number of members is unique and exists exactly then. Laws in refutation form need no such premise. Fixing every list's number up to relational indiscernibility, in every world, is equivalent to excluded middle (`World.numbers_indiscernible_iff_em`). Reading by occurrence keeps amounts and local laws but does not track occurrences across steps. Open: the connection to likeness of configurations (`Alike`), and corollaries for the other 24 uses classified as premise-free. The earlier inventory of `Distinct` uses is a historical snapshot (at commit 193c5511). Records: [listing-once-is-decided-identity](·), [relata-counted-up-to-indiscernibility](·). Lean: [Becoming.Listing](·). Study: [section 101](·).
