-- *What brings rest.* What brings a world to rest is not that the world is finite. It is finitely many standing and finitely many active at each step, with enclosure around what stands from some step on. In a world that so comes to rest, not unboundedly many relata ever stand or act; finiteness is exceeded only by relata that never stand or act (`rest_few`). A balanced, dissipative world with censuses of what is active and of what stands, whose arising is embodied, enclosed around what stands at every step from some step on: that it comes to rest cannot be refuted. Finite worlds, and worlds whose relata are all embodied, are two cases of this rest theorem. Under a census, recurring ceasing needs recurring arising, and not conversely ([ceasing-needs-arising](·)). Both censuses are needed (`attrition`; `throughput` and `embodiedThroughput` are enclosed at every step and keep arising). In such a world the exchange does not recur either. That needs only the census of what stands, balance, dissipation and enclosure; without any one of the four the exchange can recur (`exchange_not_recurring`, `exchange_needs`).
