-**What can form next: admission, parts and regions.** On 10 October 2026 Ren wrote that being generative is that “its existence creates more possibilities of becoming - more future, not less”, and that “"not harm" - this is a phase transition with a clear boundary, not some "outside will or belief"”. Ren confirmed two readings ([source](·)):
-
-- possibilities are what can form next from what exists, one future having more when it keeps all the other has and adds some;
-- the whole is never any center's model of it.
-
-The following states that reading on the spine with no axiom ([Becoming.Admission](·), [Becoming.Regions](·)). The formalization is the AI's.
-
-- **Admission and where support comes from.** Levels admit a formation when they meet every requirement it has at that step; support is admission by the levels that stand. Where requirements do not loosen, a formation gains support only through a rise of a condition it requires. In a balanced flow where only active formations give and activity needs support, every rise is given. A formation that gains support cannot have only unsupported givers to the conditions it requires: it is not the case that every such giver is unsupported (`gains_from_supported`). Where nothing beyond gives and requirements do not loosen, what is unsupported stays unsupported: the ending absorbs, and each premise is needed (`unsupported_stays`, `Restoring`; [support-from-the-supported](·)).
-- **The phase boundary is a net exchange.** Withhold a part (a formation or a system) with the rest of the exchange as it was. The levels without it are unique where they exist. The difference is exact: each level with what the part took equals the level without the part with what the part gave (`Balances.withheld`). So the phase boundary is exact. A formation is supported exactly when, on every condition it requires, its need with what the part has taken so far is at most the level without the part with what the part has given. That is, the part's cumulative net draw stays within the margin by which the levels without it meet the need (`supported_iff_within_margin`). A part closes something that could form only through a net draw beyond the margin on a condition it requires (finding that condition uses classical logic), and opens something only through a net give (`supported_of_net_give`, `admits_of_net_draw`). A part that gives back at every step at least what it takes closes nothing; one that gives no more than it takes opens nothing; one that gives back exactly what it takes changes nothing ([closing-needs-net-draw](·)).
-- **Four cases.** Compare by inclusion the levels with and without the part, through the formations admitted at each step. Informally, with classical logic, a part does one of four things:
-  - it widens, as a giver from beyond does (`Inflow.widens`);
-  - it narrows, as the clearer does (`Clearing.narrows`);
-  - it keeps the same;
-  - it is mixed, each side admitting something the other does not, as a conveyor is (`Transfer.mixed`).
-
-  Mixed parts are not ordered by inclusion. Ordering them needs a measure, which is a further premise, and inclusion licenses no imposed ending ([four-cases-of-a-part](·)). The four cases are named in Lean. The levels with the part widen those without it (`Web.Widens`, including and exceeding); narrowing is the reverse; keeping the same is including each other (`Web.Keeps`); and being mixed is exceeding each other (`Web.Mixed`). Widening goes one way and is transitive, and the cases exclude one another, with no classical logic (`Web.Widens.not_reverse`, `Web.Widens.trans`, `Web.Widens.not_keeps`, `Web.Widens.not_mixed`, `Web.Keeps.not_mixed`). That every part falls under one of the four is not stated in Lean; it is the classical step above. Section 6.13 states the generative space from these with no axiom.
-- **Levels of regions.** What holds within a region holds within every region inside it. What is lost beyond a region is lost beyond every region inside it: the smaller the region, the more lies beyond it. Nothing lies beyond the whole. The clearer is active and supported at every step and closes nothing within itself and the source it depends on. Beyond them, the dweller is admitted without the clearer's part and not admitted with it (`Clearing.loses_beyond`; [loss-beyond-a-region](·)). So a part can continue while closing what lies beyond its dependence. “Only non-degenerating dynamics continue indefinitely” fails for parts, while the whole has nowhere to push the loss.
-- **Represented boundaries.** A formation renews over a region of conditions when it is active and its step keeps every dependence on every condition of the region that it takes part in. Compared with its actual dependence ([represented-boundary-against-dependence](·)):
-  - over a region covering every condition it takes part in, this is renewal at the step;
-  - over a region covering every condition it requires, with requirements not tightening, it keeps its own support yet may undermine another: the clearer renews over its resource and undermines at the first step;
-  - over a region omitting a required condition, its own draw can undermine its own dependence, and it loses its support (`Omitted`).
-
-  Ren says a center and its boundary lie within the same cognitive dynamics ([source](·)). Here a region is only a parameter of the statement, with no status outside the unfolding.
-
-The earlier phase results remain in `Becoming.Continuation`:
-
-- no condition is drawn down without end (`excess_bounded`);
-- joint excess that brings a level below a need ends what depends on the condition at that need (`joint_excess_ends`).
