-**How a carried difference grows.** `Becoming.Propagation` gives the most difference that can arrive, with witnesses only for loops ([Becoming.Loops](·)). The carried amount expands by order: order j (`carriedWithin`) is what routes of at most j responses carry, each order being what the strengths make of the order below (`carriedFrom`); the orders rise with j to the carried amount (`carriedWithin_mono`, `carriedWithin_le_carried`), and order j is exact up to step j (`carriedWithin_exact`), as each step adds at most one response. The level of one condition is coupled into another (`Couples`) where at some step a formation present, outside the part, exchanges with the second with positive strength on that level; a chain of couplings (`Coupled`) leads from one condition to another, a loop (`OnLoop`) back to where it started, and e lies upstream of c (`Upstream`) where a chain leads from e to c. Where no chain of j + 1 couplings ends at c, order j is exact at c at every step (`carriedWithin_exact_of_not_coupledInto`); with every condition listed, a chain as long as the list passes some condition twice (`loop_of_coupledInto`, by pigeonhole), so where no loop lies upstream of c, the order one less than the number of conditions is exact at c at every step (`carriedWithin_exact_of_no_loop`). Where the couplings hold at every step (`CouplesAlways`), what reaches a loop upstream keeps arriving at c by longer routes, so every order leaves something out (`carriedWithin_lt_of_loop`): a finite order is exact from every starting gap exactly where no loop lies upstream (`finite_order_iff_no_loop`); the couplings must hold at every step, as a loop coupled at the first step only leaves order one exact (`Loops.Once.not_always`). Along a chain with strengths at least w, what reaches its start arrives at its end times the product of the strengths (`carried_along`); around a loop, what has reached it returns multiplied by at least one plus the product of the strengths around it (`carried_around`), and after R times around by that factor to the power R (`carried_runaway`). With the part giving no gap after the start, starting gaps at most D and the strengths into each condition (`inflow`) summing to at most M at each step: order j carries at most D times the sum over i ≤ j of the ways to place i responses among t steps (`Loops.binom`) times M^i, a polynomial in t of degree j (`carriedWithin_le_withinBound`, `Loops.withinBound_eq`); the carried amount is at most D(1 + M)^t (`carried_le_geomBound`); and a cut at order j misses at most their difference (`carried_le_carriedWithin_add`). One condition returning its level to itself with strength M, a self-loop, attains all three exactly (`Loops.SelfLoop.attained`), so no smaller bound follows from D and M alone. Where no loop lies upstream, the carried amount is at most a polynomial in the steps, of degree one less than the number of conditions (`carried_le_polynomial`). With the same formations and strengths at every step (`stationary`), every coupling holds at every step, every loop at least doubles what reaches it every time around, since a positive natural-number strength is at least one (`carried_doubles_around`), and what reaches a loop upstream of c arrives at c so doubled (`carried_doubles_into`); a doubling outgrows every polynomial (`Loops.not_poly_of_doubling`), so the carried amount is bounded by a polynomial exactly where no loop lies upstream of c (`polynomial_iff_no_loop`). By `most_difference_arrives` these are statements about every law with the strengths: where no loop lies upstream, the most difference that can arrive is what the finite order carries (`most_difference_within`), and every gap is at most the polynomial (`gap_le_polynomial`); with the same formations and strengths at every step, the most difference that can arrive is bounded by a polynomial exactly where no loop lies upstream (`gap_polynomial_iff_no_loop`). *Readings* (the AI's, agreed by Ren on 16 October 2026; [source](·)): on 12 October Ren compared the theory with quantum dynamics and "Fermi's diagrams": “there are so many patterns and loops but we can still find a way to to integrate so we can still calculate this effects but how how accurate can we do that uh what is the limitation do we have losses” ([source](·)); Ren meant Feynman diagrams, and the match is structural, not physical. The integration Ren asked about, for laws that are not determined, is the sum over histories of `Becoming.Weighted` with its four losses; the orders here are the same structural kind, organized by the number of responses as Feynman diagrams are by their vertices, `Loops.binom` counting their time orderings; the two are not yet joined (they are now joined by `Becoming.Routes`: the orders are sums over routes back in time, and a cut is a truncation). The accuracy of a cut is the rest of the sum, and it is attained, the limit for what the strengths and the starting gaps alone allow; where no loop lies upstream a finite order loses nothing, and where one does, coupled at every step, every finite order loses something and what reaches the loop grows at least geometrically. Only a loop makes a difference run away, where running away means growing faster than every polynomial, and of the most difference that can arrive, not of what every law does: without a loop a held difference keeps arriving but grows at most polynomially (`Propagation.Chain` is of degree one), and with a loop that couples at every step it grows at least geometrically (`Propagation.Loop` is the self-loop of strength one); so the most difference that can arrive grows faster than every polynomial only through a loop, and, with natural-number strengths, each positive one at least one, through every loop that couples at every step (narrowed and taken on 18 October 2026, D43: with real strengths below one such a loop need not run away, which is damping), couplings that hold only at some steps being open here. Which loops a world has is part of its situation (Ren, 16 October: “the situation makes it true”): loops form and dissolve within the unfolding, and the theorems take them as they stand at each step; the strengths are supplied. This reading is taken for now, open to revision once a theorem states how loops form within the unfolding, for example where a formation's drawing comes to respond to the level its drawing lowers, where this meets `Becoming.Runaway` (the loop-formation theorem is now `Becoming.Closing`, and Ren agreed to the reading as revised: which loops a world has is part of its situation: loops form within the unfolding by closing, a loop forming at the step at which some exchange comes to respond to a level downstream of the condition it gives to, and dissolve where their couplings stop (once the schedule has settled, a finite order is exact exactly where no loop of the couplings that go on lies upstream); where the strengths are read off the course, which loops form and when is read off the unfolding; the theorems take the loops as they stand at each step, the strengths or the way they are read being supplied; [source](·)). The strengths themselves, how fast a runaway is beyond the product of the strengths, infinitely many conditions, continuous time and real amounts are not derived; couplings that come and go are treated in `Becoming.Remainder`. See [how-a-carried-difference-grows](·).
