-**What a cut loses, for every schedule of couplings.** The equivalence of `Becoming.Loops` needs every coupling to hold at every step and every condition listed, and a loop coupled at the first step only shows that the first cannot be dropped as it stands ([Becoming.Remainder](·)). Here what a cut loses, and where, is stated for every schedule of couplings and any list of conditions. What one response carries from given levels (`responded`) is the carrying law with no starting gap and no gap of the part; j responses in turn (`responses`) respond each to what the one before carried. The carried amount is the orders below j plus what j responses in turn carry from the carried amount itself (`carried_eq_orderBelow_add`), so a cut at order j loses exactly what j + 1 responses carry from it (`carried_eq_carriedWithin_add`); the proof only adds, as giving is additive in the levels read (`carriedGive_add`, `carriedFrom_add`). The loss falls as the order rises (`responses_antitone`), is at most the bound of `Becoming.Loops` for every schedule (`responses_le_missBound`), and is exactly that bound for the self-loop (`Loops.SelfLoop.responses_eq`). One response carries something to c by step t exactly where, at a step before t, the level of a condition is coupled into c (`CouplesAt`) and positive (`responded_pos_iff`); a gap is conveyed to c through j responses or more (`ConveyedThrough`) where the route in time of `Becoming.Propagation` (`Conveyed`) reaches a condition and j couplings carry it on to c, each reading a level the one before had reached. The orders below j miss something at c at step t exactly where a gap is conveyed there through j responses or more (`orderBelow_lt_iff`), and order j is exact there exactly where none is conveyed through j + 1 (`carriedWithin_eq_iff`). A chain of j couplings in time order ends at c (`CoupledIntoBy`) where each coupling reads a level the one before had reached: order j is exact from every start exactly where no such chain of j + 1 couplings ends there (`exact_every_start_iff`), and a finite order is exact at c at every step from every start exactly where the chains in time order into c are bounded in length (`finite_order_iff_bounded`), one order then serving every start (`finite_order_uniform_iff`). These need no premise. A coupling recurs (`CouplingRecurs`) where it reads levels at steps as late as one likes: where a gap reaches a loop of recurring couplings and recurring couplings lead from it to c, every order misses something at c (`carriedWithin_lt_of_recurring_loop`); where no coupling reads a level from step N on, order N is exact everywhere (`exact_of_reading_before`). Each coupling reads a listed condition, so a chain one longer than the list passes a loop, with no listing of every condition (`loop_of_coupledInto_succ`). The strengths from step N on (`strengthsFrom`) drop every reading of a level before N; a coupling recurs exactly where it is a coupling from every step on (`couplingRecurs_iff`); of a chain in time order of N + j couplings, the last j are a chain of the couplings from step N on (`CoupledIntoBy.tail`). A schedule has settled by step N (`SettledFrom`) where every coupling from N on recurs: then a finite order is exact at c from every start exactly where no loop of the couplings from step N on lies upstream of c (`finite_order_iff_no_loop_from`), with no listing of every condition; whatever the schedule, where no such loop lies upstream the carried amount is at most a polynomial in the steps (`carried_le_polynomial_from`). Couplings at every step have settled from the first step (`settledFrom_zero_of_always`), so `finite_order_iff_no_loop` holds with no listing of every condition (`Remainder.finite_order_iff_no_loop`); `Loops.Once` reads the first step only, has no chain of two couplings in time order, and its schedule has settled from the second step with nothing coupling (`Loops.Once.no_chain_of_two`, `Loops.Once.settled`). By `most_difference_arrives`, for every law with the strengths, every gap from every start is at most what order j carries exactly where no chain of j + 1 couplings in time order ends there (`gap_within_order_iff`). *Readings* [AI reading, D43]. Ren asked on 12 October 2026 “how how accurate can we do that uh what is the limitation do we have losses” ([source](·)), and on 16 October took the orders of `Becoming.Loops` as the same structural kind as the sum over histories; the exact remainder says what a cut of that expansion loses, in the form of a Neumann series's remainder: the orders below j are the terms of fewer than j responses, and a cut loses what j responses carry from the whole; as in time-ordered perturbation theory (the Dyson series), the responses of a route come in time order, and a cut loses exactly where a route in time with more responses arrives; the loss is the cut's, not the theory's, the carried amount itself being exact. What decides a cut's loss is the time order of the couplings, not loops drawn without time: the loops that matter are those of the couplings that go on, and a loop whose couplings stop is passing. The most difference that can arrive grows faster than every polynomial only through a loop of the couplings that go on, and not through every such loop: one that recurs rarely stays polynomial (`Frequency.Sparse.polynomial`, under “How often couplings act” below). That every schedule settles at some step is not derived (a coupling that does not recur stops, but whether one recurs is not decided here, and the core assumes no excluded middle); nor are how fast a runaway is beyond the product of the strengths, laws reading infinitely many conditions at a step, continuous time and real amounts (`Becoming.Frequency`, under “How often couplings act” below, answers three of these: not through every such loop; that every schedule settles is exactly a principle of reasoning; the rate counts the steps at which couplings act, the spectral radius of given strengths still not derived). S