-**The split that makes sense, and states read off a history.** Ren asked what the “make sense” of a split itself is. With the definition of `Becoming.Unfolding`, a description makes sense when it identifies exactly the alike configurations. For a determined law read through a registration, alike is the same registered future (`Law.alike_iff_sameFuture`).
-
-- The coarsest split that keeps what is registered and carries a law makes sense and loses nothing registered (`Law.identifiesAlike_of_law`, `Law.sameFuture_of_law`, [Becoming.Description](·)). Carrying a law is not enough: the split that distinguishes nothing always carries a law and makes sense only where nothing registered ever differs (`Law.const_identifiesAlike_iff`).
-- What is registered now makes sense by itself exactly where it carries a law, a condition on one step (`Law.now_identifiesAlike_iff_goAlike`). A registered part alone makes sense exactly where the rest makes no difference to its next value (`Law.part_identifiesAlike_iff_step`). Where a controller's rule changes with it, it makes sense exactly where the rule makes no difference to it (`Law.adaptive_identifiesAlike_iff`, the condition of `adaptive_state_law_iff` in `Becoming.Mechanism`). Cognition or a controller lies within the split that makes sense exactly where it makes a registered difference.
-- *States read off a history.* States are not given with configurations; they are read off a registered history. Two moments are one state when everything registered after them is alike (`SameAhead`). Every registered history is a determined law read through what is registered: the law of moments, which uses only the succession of steps (the representation's shared step order). Its alike moments are exactly the moments of one state (`alike_moments_iff`). The law of moments is the successor map on step indices, so this theorem is close to definitional.
-- So, beyond the succession of steps, that an unfolding has a law is not a premise, and a law's content is its compression. There are finitely many states exactly where the registration repeats from some moment on (`finitely_many_states_iff`, through a pigeonhole up to a relation with no decision, `shared_below`). A returning configuration has finitely many states under every registration (`Law.Returns.states`). The registration of two-valued negation has exactly two states, each defined through the other (`Negation.two_states`, `Negation.each_through_other`).
-
-Record: [the-split-that-makes-sense](·). Study: [section 94](·).
