-**Contextual registration.** Ren asked whether what the framing loses is like “wave/particle” ([source](·)). By type, the frame reads claims as free of context: what a claim registers does not depend on what is registered with it. This is named here as a commitment of the representation, with the condition under which it fits ([Becoming.Context](·)).
-
-- Claims are registered in contexts. The registrations glue exactly where any two contexts registering one claim register it alike (`Contextual.glues_iff_agree`). A frame of context-free claims fits exactly where the registrations glue at every configuration (`Contextual.fitsFree_iff`). The frame indexed by context always fits (`Contextual.indexed_fits`).
-- Some worlds glue at no configuration: Specker's three boxes and the Mermin–Peres square. Each is consistent within every context, no context forces any claim, and there is no context-free reading (`Specker.noGlobal`, `Square.noGlobal`).
-- Nine observables on two qubits realize the square. No assignment of values is jointly shown on every line by states, where states are represented as nonzero vectors with four integer components (`no_context_free_values`, [Becoming.Quantum](·)). This is narrower than the full Hilbert space; quantum registration, so represented, is of this kind.
-- Parts fail a whole in two ways, on the same three pairs: by not giving the whole back (the parity of `Becoming.Description`, `evenBoxes_not_determined`), and by allowing what no whole has (`Specker.no_whole`).
-
-*Readings* [AI reading, D33; Ren agreed with the reply containing them]. Wave and particle are both projections onto context-free values. A value is a registration in context, that is, a relation. The truth of Ren's speculation is relational. Record: [contextual-registration](·). Study: [section 96](·).
