-**Identification: what a bounded record fixes.** Ren speculated that “the full "truth" can be derived, situationally, including the cognition/controller part” ([source](·)). Ren later wrote that “reality is always situational” ([source](·)). What follows says what a finite record fixes where the situation has a bound on its number of states ([Becoming.Identification](·)).
-
-- *Two histories.* Two registered histories with at most N and N′ states that agree before N + N′ − 1 agree everywhere, so a record of that length fixes the course (`Identification.bounded_agree`). The proof goes through a two-periods lemma after Fine and Wilf, in a form counting states rather than the classical form with the greatest common divisor (`Identification.period_extends`). For every pair of bounds, no shorter record suffices (`Identification.bounded_agree_exact`).
-- *Given the bound, induction is deduction.* A lasso hypothesis within N that is not refuted on the record of length 2N − 1 is the law of the whole course (`Identification.bounded_induction`). The learner given the bound is refuted nowhere and is right at every step from 2N − 1 on, and no learner can always claim earlier (`Identification.withBound_exact`, `Identification.withBound_earliest`).
-- *Not given the bound.* The learner not given the bound (learning by refutation, `Becoming.Learning`) also rests, at a step the bound gives: it is right at every step from N + (2N − 1)·tri N on (`Identification.held_byElimination_rests`), though its record never shows this. That step is cubic in N. On two-valued negation along a cycle of N = 2m + 3 places, broken once in every turn, it is refuted at step (N³ − N)/4, each even shift it passes costing a whole turn (`Identification.byElimination_missed_late`, `Identification.byElimination_rest_cubic`). Where there is no bound, no record fixes the next value.
-- *The rest step needs no bound.* The learner by the least bound is given no bound. It claims by the first hypothesis borne out within the least bound within which the record bears out some lasso hypothesis. On every history with at most N states it is right at every step from 2N − 1 on, for every N (`Identification.leastBound_exact`). No learner, given the bound or not, is right at step 2N − 2 on every such history (`Identification.rest_earliest`). So 2N − 1 is the optimal rest step, and the bound does not move it. What the bound buys is silence: a learner right at step 1 on every one-state history cannot be refuted nowhere on every two-state history (`Identification.rest_without_bound_missed`).
-- *Controllers within the world.* A controller in the world is within what is registered. A world that answers the learner given the bound has more states than the bound (`Identification.answer_exceeds_bound`), while the liar's rhythm is derived from step 3 on (`Identification.negation_derived`).
-- *Naming by a finite future.* In a world a list holds, naming each configuration by its registered future for 2N − 1 steps makes sense and carries the law, with no quotient axiom (`Law.name_identifiesAlike`, `Law.name_carries`). Naming by the whole future would need function extensionality.
-- *Moore's bound.* Within one world the bound is sharper. Under a determined law, take a list of N members closed under the law, with the members' values compared decidably. Two members have the same registered future exactly where they agree within N − 1 steps (Moore's bound, `Law.sameFuture_iff_classes`). Agreeing within k + 1 steps is a chain of ever finer equivalences: members with different values give two classes, each strict refinement adds a class, and there are at most N classes (`Refining.steady_within` of [Becoming.Refinement](·)). The bound is exact for every N ≥ 2 (`Law.sameFuture_classes_exact`).
-- *No decision needed.* The bound needs no decision: what is decided is the positions, not the values. Read the law on the positions of the list, whose equality is settled. Merge, pair by pair, the positions that agreements in the record join; this gives a labelling of the positions in which each label has one value. Moore's bound holds for the labelling and carries back to the values (`Law.sameFuture_iff_within`, no premise). The same holds for two moments of one history (`Identification.sameAhead_iff_within`).
-- *Tests.* Tests are bounded records too. Under a determined law read through a registration, in a world of N configurations, two configurations pass the same tests (`SameTests` of `Becoming.Histories`) exactly where their registered futures agree within N − 1 steps. This is exact for a world wholly held by N configurations. Between two worlds the bound is N + N′ − 1, exact for every pair ([Becoming.Distinguishing](·): `Law.sameTests_iff_within`, `frameBy_within_exact`, `Law.sameTests_iff_below_across`, `frameBy_across_exact`). Between frames whose moves carry the registration, likeness to depth k is exactly agreement within k steps, so the depth N + N′ − 1 between branching frames is also exact for every pair (`labelled_across_exact`). Read as one world, two histories have Moore's bound N + N′ − 1, the bound of Fine and Wilf.
-
-“Situationally” is not read as the bound, since Ren wrote that reality is always situational (15 October 2026). The bound is the condition under which a situation's finite record fixes everything registered. *Reading* [AI reading, D34]: a finite record is a projection, and the bound makes it lose nothing of the course. Record: [identification-where-bounded](·). Study: [section 97](·).
