-**What a learner can learn through its registration.** A learner reads what a registration registers and claims what it does not register. Take a registration `reg` of configurations and a target `val`. A learner claims at step t from the registered record before t, and rests on the target from step T when it claims the target at every step from T on ([Becoming.Learnable](·)).
-
-- *No premise.* Two configurations with the same registered future give every learner the same record at every step (`prefixes_same`). So at a step where their targets differ, a claim is right on at most one of them (`claim_not_right_on_both`). With the target's values decidable, it is refuted on one, while the two records are equal everywhere and do not show which (`missed_on_one`).
-- A learner resting on both has targets that agree from some step on (`agree_of_rests_both`). Where the targets differ at arbitrarily late steps, no learner rests on both, already where the law leaves both configurations unchanged (`not_rests_on_both`, `still_not_rests_on_both`).
-- Where the target is read off the registration (`val s = g (reg s)`), the lasso learner reading registered values is given neither the configurations, nor their number, nor any value of the target. In every world a list holds, it rests from the identification bound on (`rests_through`).
-- *Exactly.* In a world a list holds, some learner reading the registration rests at every configuration exactly where configurations with the same registered future have targets agreeing from some step on (`learnable_iff`). The necessity side holds of every learner, whatever it is given. The learner on the sufficiency side is given the world and the target (`fromLaw`); so this is an identifiability condition, not a learning procedure that finds the target unaided.
-- Targets that go alike across the registration (`Lumped`) are always learnable (`learnable_of_lumped`). Where the law leaves every configuration unchanged, these are exactly the learnable targets (`still_learnable_iff_lumped`): the lumping through which `Becoming.Learning` states generalization.
-- *Given nothing, the future is fixed* ([Becoming.Unrolling](·), [what-an-extrapolation-given-nothing-fixes](·)). Unroll the lasso learner given nothing (the extrapolation by elimination over the lasso candidates) on its own proposals, putting each proposed value at the front of the prefix. In every world a list holds, from the identification bound on, it then proposes the value through the map at every step ahead (`Learnable.ahead_fixes`). Over every course no prefix fixes the next value (`Learning.no_prefix_fixes_next`); here a list holds the world.
-- *Learnable exactly where a reading of the future.* A target is learnable exactly where some reading of futures, respecting values at each step, is the target from some step on along every orbit (`Learnable.learnable_iff_reading`). The reading on the necessity side is given the world, the law and the target (`Learnable.fromWorld`): a configuration as many steps on as the list is long returns, and returning configurations whose targets agree from some step on have equal targets (`Learnable.eq_of_returns`). On the sufficiency side the learner given only the reading learns the target (`Learnable.reading_rests_from`).
-- *Given nothing, not every learnable target.* No learner given nothing rests on every learnable target: in a one-configuration world the targets `true` and `false` are each learnable, with the same prefixes (`Learnable.given_nothing_not_every`). The same three hold along the runs of the law (`LearnableRuns.ahead_fixes`, `LearnableRuns.learnable_iff_reading`, `LearnableRuns.given_nothing_not_every`).
-- *What finitely many labels fix* ([Becoming.Labels](·), [what-finitely-many-labels-fix](·)). A table of labelled names is finite: a name is a finite future through the map, as `Law.name` writes it, and a label is a value of the feature. The extrapolation given a table unrolls the extrapolation given nothing and proposes the label of the first name its proposed future begins with (`Learnable.byTable`). It is given neither the configurations, nor how many there are, nor the law. In a world a list of N holds, take each configuration as many steps on as the list is long, name it by its first N − 1 values through the map (Moore's bound, `Law.sameFuture_iff_within`) and label it with its feature. This table of the world rests in accord with every learnable feature from the bound of the extrapolation given nothing on (`Learnable.table_rests`). So a feature is learnable exactly where the extrapolation given some table of at most N labelled names of N − 1 values learns it (`Learnable.learnable_iff_table`). Every value the feature takes once it rests is a label (`Learnable.label_of_rests`). Where the map also carries the feature, the extrapolation given nothing learns it, and the labels before the bound fix every later one (`Learnable.rests_with_labels`, `Learnable.ahead_with_labels`). The same holds along the runs of the law (`LearnableRuns.table_rests`, `LearnableRuns.learnable_iff_table`, `LearnableRuns.labels_of_still`, `LearnableRuns.rests_with_labels`, `LearnableRuns.ahead_with_labels`).
-
-*Readings* [AI reading, D26]. Registration bounds what is learned. This is the not-seeing below threshold of `Becoming.Testing`, stated for learners; what lies beyond registration is reached only by widening it. Record: [learnable-through-registration](·). Study: [section 119](·).
