-**Identification where runs part, and typicality by counting.** The identification line is stated for determined laws. Ren, 15 October 2026: “"What this line doesn’t reach" is also worth doing” ([source](·)). A process fixes only which steps are admissible, and from one configuration its runs can go different ways. Two runs part at a step where they register the same before it and differ at it; their records before it are one record, so no learner, whatever it is given, the world included, is right on both (`Parting.not_right_on_both`, [Becoming.Parting](·)). Where no two runs from a configuration part, not parting is kept along runs; on a run held by a list of N configurations the registered course has at most N states, and the learner by the least bound, given nothing, is right from N + N − 1 on, and no sooner (`Parting.course_statesBelow`, `Parting.leastBound_right`, `Parting.rest_earliest_runs`). So the identification line holds for every process exactly where runs do not part: a determined law is an instance (`Parting.unparted_of_determined`), and so is a branching that does not show in what is registered (`Parting.free_unparted_const`). Where runs part, the bound bounds nothing: under free branching every course is a run's course, after every record two runs part, so no record shows that a world will not part; every learner has a run on which it is never right; and a run on two configurations has a course with no bound on its states (`Parting.free_parts_after`, `Parting.free_never_right`, `Parting.free_unbounded`). Nor does a record fix weights: it registers a course, not a weight. With two values counted by natural weights p and q, the weighted squared deviations (q·a − p·b)² of the courses of n steps add up exactly to n·p·q·(p + q)ⁿ, so the courses deviating by at least d weigh, times d, at most that (`Typicality.spread`, `Typicality.typical`, [Becoming.Typicality](·)): most courses show the weights, the counting form of the law of large numbers, with no premise; the weights are `Becoming.Weighted`'s counting weights of free branching (`Typicality.sum_courses_weight`). That the course which happens is among the most is not derived: every course is a run. Of the two AI readings first given here (“at a parting the branch is settled only when taken”; “reading weights as chances adds the premise that the course which happens is typical”), Ren wrote on 16 October 2026: “we are describing what everything obeys, but at every moment, the actual change depends on the situation … just saying we are all one doesn't make it true, the situation makes it true” ([source](·)). Read so (the AI's reading of Ren's words, which Ren took on 16 October 2026: “yes, i take those readings”): everything registered is derived situationally, as far as runs do not part; at a parting neither the record nor the supplied law fixes which way the run goes, and the situation fixes it; weights count situations, as the next paragraph states. Records: [identification-where-runs-part](·), [typicality-by-counting](·). Study: [section 140](·).
