+### 4.3 Relative position, and what a collective's continuation needs
+
+**In words.** A part's position read against the others' sums to zero by counting: its rise is the rest's summed fall, and no change raises every part. Position reads the amounts only up to one amount common to every part, so it bounds what is drawn in all from below, by the spread, and never from above. Position does not protect: a joint excess ends a part at its end through its dependence, whatever its position. A collective's continuation is asked of runs, not of positions: keeping a part is not renewal, there is no number of the whole, conditions are joined rather than multiplied, a counter-loop has to reach how the rule reads, and the invariants are laws, not axioms.
+
