+**Why the core excludes `propext`, and why the periphery may use it** ([`Becoming.PropositionIdentity`](·), no axiom; [record](·)). From the relational start a proposition is read through its relations of derivation, what derives it and what it derives, and `propext` (`(a ↔ b) → a = b`) says that a proposition is nothing beyond them. The module states this exactly. `propext`, as a proposition, holds exactly where every reading of propositions is lumped by interderivability (`Learning.Lumped`, read through the two ends of an interderivation; `extensional_iff_lumped`), and exactly where the reading that reads a proposition by name, the identity, is (`byName_lumped_iff`); a reading built from relations (atoms, the connectives, quantifiers over any type) respects interderivability already, with `↔` rather than `=` (`Formula.eval_congr`). The identification forgets no witness: any two witnesses of `a ↔ b` are equal (`iff_witness_unique`), while `Bool` has two different self-inverse maps (`two_equivalences`), so identifying types along an equivalence would lose which one. So `propext` is an exact lumping of outcomes. What it does not keep is the course: transport along any equation of propositions with a constant motive keeps the value (`transport_const`), but a value transported along `propext` does not reduce. `#reduce` stops at the transport, and neither `rfl` nor `decide` proves the value it provably has; this is a kernel observation with Lean 4.29.1, kept with the record, not a theorem. The core's runs, censuses and witnesses are checked by computation (`rfl` and `decide` on them), so the core states `↔` wherever a result would need `=` between propositions and keeps its empty report; the periphery, whose results concern outcomes and carry their own receipts, may use `propext` as the exact lumping it is. That every reading of a proposition is relational is `propext` itself: here it is a hypothesis (`Extensional`), never a conclusion.
